{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2714","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2714","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2714\",\"parent_revision\":\"f22183180c6b61f3b075058800d1369b65d4ef86\",\"root_revision\":\"da9af16c44cf11627b6b2c9f3f1df98a4635199b3d1fd55781425ff2428748e6\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe DH workflow graph schema documents a `gap` edge type (marking an unattested/unverified transition) as required for correct impact-radius analysis, but the graph assembler has no code path that can ever produce one \u2014 the relevant construction parameters are never set to non-default values, and the gap count in assembled output is always zero.\\n\\nWhere observed: PR #2626 code review, `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` and `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md`.\\n\\nImpact: impact-radius analysis over unattested/unverified transitions in the workflow graph has zero coverage, despite the schema documenting this as a first-class, required concept.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T05:55:35.177087Z</sub>\\n\\nCLAIM 1: \\\"_edge() function at line 437 has gap: bool = False parameter that is never set to True in any call site\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: pr-2626:plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py line 437\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content: Function signature shows `gap: bool = False,` parameter defined\\n  - All 9 call sites (lines 508, 520, 532, 546, 601, 627, 658, 689, 717) checked \u2014 none pass `gap=True`; parameter uses default value throughout\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: pr-2626 branch; grep on all _edge() invocations\\n  - Finding: Zero instances of `gap=True` passed to _edge() function\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The gap parameter exists in _edge() function signature with default value False, but no call site in the codebase explicitly sets it to True, confirming the parameter is defined but unused.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 2: \\\"gap_count is hardcoded to 0 in assembled output (line 938)\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: pr-2626:plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py line 938\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content: `\\\"gap_count\\\": 0,` appears in assembled graph output\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: Searched entire file for gap_count occurrences\\n  - Finding: Single hardcoded occurrence at line 938\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The gap_count field in the assembled graph output is hardcoded to 0 with no logic to calculate the actual number of gap edges.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 3: \\\"graph-schema.md documents gap edges as first-class concept with dashed amber rendering, verified=false, gap=true\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: pr-2626:plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md lines 181-182, 214, 393\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content: \\n    - Line 181-182: \\\"Every edge carries `verified: true|false` and `gap: true|false`. A gap edge is rendered differently in the explorer (dashed, amber).\\\"\\n    - Line 214: Edge type `gap` defined as \\\"transition exists but is unattested in source\\\"\\n    - Line 393: \\\"Every edge of type `gap` sets `gap: true` and `verified: false`\\\"\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: Graph schema example JSON (line 192) shows edge structure with both verified and gap fields\\n  - Finding: Documentation consistently describes gap edges as documented first-class edge type with specified rendering (dashed, amber) and required field values\\n\\nEXPLANATION: Documentation defines gap edges with explicit rendering rules (dashed amber), field constraints (verified=false, gap=true), and semantic meaning (unattested transitions).\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 4: \\\"OVERLAYS dict at line 776 exists but no gap edges are produced\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: pr-2626:plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py line 776\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content: OVERLAYS dict spans lines 776-810; six overlays defined (workflow, agents, instructions, tools, artifacts, concurrency)\\n  - Each overlay specifies edge_types list: none include \\\"gap\\\" in their edge_types:\\n    - workflow: [\\\"branch\\\", \\\"routes_to\\\"]\\n    - agents: [\\\"branch\\\", \\\"dispatches\\\"]\\n    - instructions: [\\\"branch\\\"]\\n    - tools: [\\\"calls\\\", \\\"stores_in\\\"]\\n    - artifacts: [\\\"reads\\\", \\\"writes\\\"]\\n    - concurrency: [\\\"branch\\\", \\\"dispatches\\\"]\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: All _edge() call sites produce edge types in the above lists; no call site creates edge_type=\\\"gap\\\"\\n  - Finding: Edge filtering excludes \\\"gap\\\" from all view overlays; edge production functions (build_branch_edges, build_dispatches_edges, etc.) never call _edge with type=\\\"gap\\\"\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The OVERLAYS configuration excludes gap edges from all six visualization overlays, and the assembler functions never produce gap-type edges, making gap edges inaccessible to the explorer even if gap_count were accurate.\\n\\n---\\n\\nCLAIM 5: \\\"dh-workflow-explorer.html would render gap edges as dashed amber if they existed in the output JSON\\\"\\nVERDICT: VERIFIED\\nEVIDENCE:\\n  - Source: pr-2626:plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html lines 521, 550, 555-556\\n  - Retrieved: 2026-07-06\\n  - Content:\\n    - Line 521: HTML legend item shows gap edge with dashed line and #ff6d00 (amber) color\\n    - Line 550: `gap: '#ff6d00'` color mapping\\n    - Line 555: `const EDGE_DASHED = new Set(['reads', 'gap', 'stores_in']);` marks gap for dashed rendering\\n    - Line 556: `const EDGE_THICK = new Set(['gap']);` marks gap for thick line weight\\n\\nCROSS_CHECK:\\n  - Source: CSS classes for dashed styling (line 424: `.legend-edge-line.dashed`) and JavaScript rendering logic\\n  - Finding: Rendering pipeline checks edge.type against EDGE_DASHED set and applies dashed style; color #ff6d00 (orange/amber) applied to gap edges\\n\\nEXPLANATION: The explorer HTML contains complete rendering logic for gap edges with dashed amber styling; if gap edges were present in the output JSON, they would render correctly as dashed amber lines.\\n\\n---\\n\\nSUMMARY:\\nAll five claims are VERIFIED. The evidence confirms that:\\n1. Gap parameter exists but is never set to True (unused in code)\\n2. Gap count is hardcoded to 0 (inconsistent with documentation)\\n3. Graph schema documents gap edges as first-class with proper rendering spec\\n4. OVERLAYS dict exists but excludes gap from all visualization views\\n5. Explorer HTML rendering logic is complete for gap edges if they existed in data\\n\\nThis validates the issue: gap edges are fully documented and the visualization is ready, but the implementation is incomplete \u2014 no edges are ever produced with type=\\\"gap\\\" or gap=true.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-07-06T06:02:04.424289Z</sub>\\n\\n**Goal**: Implement the gap edge type in assemble_graph.py so that unattested/unverified workflow transitions are surfaced in the graph and available for impact-radius analysis.\\n\\n**Assessed**: 2026-07-06T06:00:00Z (Wave 2 Final)\\n\\n**Conditions**:\\n\\n| # | Condition | State | Evidence or derivation path |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | _edge() function has gap parameter | AVAILABLE | pr-2626:assemble_graph.py line 437: `gap: bool = False` parameter exists in signature |\\n| 2 | gap_count is hardcoded to 0 in assembled output | AVAILABLE | pr-2626:assemble_graph.py line 938: `\\\"gap_count\\\": 0` hardcoded; fact-checker VERIFIED |\\n| 3 | OVERLAYS dict exists but excludes gap | AVAILABLE | pr-2626:assemble_graph.py lines 776-810; six overlays defined; none include \\\"gap\\\" in edge_types; fact-checker VERIFIED |\\n| 4 | graph-schema.md documents gap edge semantics | AVAILABLE | pr-2626:graph-schema.md lines 181-182, 214, 393; gap edges documented with dashed amber rendering, verified=false, gap=true; fact-checker VERIFIED |\\n| 5 | dh-workflow-explorer.html has complete gap rendering | AVAILABLE | pr-2626:dh-workflow-explorer.html lines 521, 550, 555-556; gap badge, filter, dashed amber CSS, gap node styling all present; fact-checker VERIFIED; impact-analyst confirmed rendering ready |\\n| 6 | Zero test coverage for gap edge generation | AVAILABLE | No test files exist for assemble_graph.py in pr-2626 or main branch; impact-analyst confirmed zero coverage |\\n| 7 | What constitutes an unattested transition (semantics) | AVAILABLE | graph-schema.md documents \\\"transition exists but is unattested in source\\\"; root-cause analysis: missing code detects unattested transitions; schema defines semantics |\\n| 8 | Gap detection approach (heuristic vs new extraction pass) | MISSING | Impact-analyst scope expansion finding: 10 upstream JSON files (L0-forks.json, L1-trace\u00d74, G1-G8\u00d78) carry NO attested/unattested markers on individual transitions. Implementation requires design decision: (a) structural heuristic within assemble_graph.py (e.g., source_file=null signal unattested), OR (b) new extraction pass by workflow-extractor to add `attested: bool` field per transition. Decision determines whether this is a single-file fix or multi-file scope expansion including workflow-extractor agent and data file schema changes. |\\n| 9 | Whether upstream L1 trace files need schema changes | DERIVABLE | Impact-analyst verified: L1-trace-work-backlog-item.json, L1-trace-complete-implementation.json, L1-trace-work-milestone.json, L1-trace-feature-skills.json all present in workflow-layers/; read each file to confirm current field set and check whether attested/unattested markers are present. If not present and approach (b) is chosen, schema must be expanded. |\\n| 10 | Whether upstream G-layer annotation files need schema changes | DERIVABLE | Impact-analyst verified: G1-G8.json files (G2-artifacts.json, G4-concurrency.json, G8-backend-routing.json confirmed) present in workflow-layers/; examine each to confirm per-transition fields and whether attestation status is recorded. If not present and approach (b) is chosen, schema updates may be needed. |\\n| 11 | Whether workflow-extractor agent instructions require updates | DERIVABLE | If gap detection approach is (b) new extraction pass: workflow-extractor.md must be updated to extract and emit `attested: bool` per transition in L1 traces. If approach (a) structural heuristic: no agent update needed. Derivable once approach is selected. |\\n| 12 | Whether dh-workflow-graph.json (committed artifact) needs regeneration | AVAILABLE | Impact-analyst confirmed: dh-workflow-graph.json is committed to main, served to dh-workflow-explorer.html, currently carries `\\\"gap_count\\\": 0` with no gap edges or gaps array entries. After assemble_graph.py is fixed, file must be regenerated as post-fix step. |\\n| 13 | Impact of gap edge implementation on existing consumers | AVAILABLE | Impact-analyst confirmed: dh-workflow-explorer.html is sole consumer; gap rendering fully implemented; will work correctly once gap edges exist in JSON; no consumer code changes required. |\\n\\n**Counts**: AVAILABLE 10, DERIVABLE 3, MISSING 1\\n\\n**Verdict**: BLOCKED\\n\\n**Missing Conditions Requiring Human Decision**:\\n\\n**Condition #8 \u2014 Gap detection approach (heuristic vs new extraction pass)** is MISSING and BLOCKS planning.\\n\\nThe impact-analyst discovered that the 10 upstream JSON data files (L0-forks.json, 4 L1-trace files, 8 G-layer annotation files) contain **no attested/unattested markers** on individual transitions. This means the implementation cannot proceed without first deciding HOW to detect unattested transitions:\\n\\n- **Option A (Structural Heuristic)**: Within assemble_graph.py, implement detection logic that infers unattested transitions from structural signals (e.g., transitions missing `source_file`/`source_heading` citations). Scope: single-file fix to assemble_graph.py only. No upstream data schema changes. No workflow-extractor agent updates.\\n\\n- **Option B (New Extraction Pass)**: Have workflow-extractor agent emit an `attested: bool` field on each L1 trace transition during the next extraction pass, then assemble_graph.py uses this field to detect and generate gap edges. Scope: assemble_graph.py + workflow-extractor.md agent instructions + L1 trace data files (4 files) + possibly G-layer files (8 files). Requires re-extraction of workflow traces.\\n\\nThis choice is a **design decision**, not something the planner can derive from the codebase. The schema documents gap edges as required but does not specify the detection mechanism. No code evidence or test can answer this \u2014 it requires a human decision about the project's philosophy for data quality and attestation tracking.\\n\\n**Changes from Wave 1 snapshot**:\\n\\n- Condition #5: Added from impact-analyst findings (explorer HTML rendering verified complete)\\n- Condition #6: Added from impact-analyst findings (zero test coverage)\\n- Conditions #8-13: NEW from impact-analyst scope expansion. Condition #8 is MISSING and BLOCKED. Conditions #9-11 are DERIVABLE once #8 is decided.\\n\\n**Recommendation**:\\n\\nBefore planning can proceed, answer: **Which gap detection approach aligns with the project's design intent \u2014 heuristic inference within assemble_graph.py (Option A), or extraction-time attestation tracking via workflow-extractor (Option B)?**\\n\\nOnce answered, conditions #9-11 become DERIVABLE and the plan can be produced with the appropriate scope and dependencies.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` (pr-2626 branch) `::_edge()` \u2014 declares `gap: bool = False` parameter that is never set to `True` in any call site; gap edges are structurally possible but never instantiated. Three distinct defects must be fixed: (1) `gap=True` must be passed for unattested transitions, (2) `gap_count` hardcoded to `0` at line 938 must be computed from actual gap edges, (3) the top-level `gaps: [...Gap]` list in the output JSON must be populated with Gap objects per the schema. Additionally, the gap-detection logic itself (identifying which transitions are unattested) must be implemented \u2014 the `OVERLAYS` dict at line 776 produces overlay views but no gap-edge detection pass. | Risk: HIGH | Why: core broken implementation \u2014 three separate defect planes, no existing code path ever produces a gap edge, and the G-layer JSON data sources (L0/L1 traces, G1-G8 annotations) currently carry no attested/unattested markers on individual transitions, so gap detection logic must be designed from scratch.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` \u2014 full gap rendering infrastructure is already implemented: `#gap-badge` counter via `updateGapBadge(g)` reading `g.edges.filter(e => e.gap).length`, `applyGapFilter()` toggle, `EDGE_DASHED` and `EDGE_THICK` sets include `'gap'`, CSS selectors `edge.gap-edge` (dashed orange, 2.5px) and `node.gap-stub` (dashed border, italic), gap filter button event listener. The badge permanently shows \\\"0 gaps\\\" until the assembler produces gap edges. The gap overlay filter button is permanently non-functional. No code change is needed to the explorer \u2014 it will work correctly once the assembler produces gap edges. | Risk: LOW | Why: consumer is correctly implemented and renders `e.gap` flag from JSON; the feature is dead only because the producer never sets `e.gap=true`.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-graph.json` (main branch, committed artifact) \u2014 the assembled graph output currently carries `\\\"gap_count\\\": 0` in its `meta` block and has no gap edges and no `gaps` array entries. This file is the JSON served to `dh-workflow-explorer.html`. After assemble_graph.py is fixed, this file must be regenerated to populate gap edges and the `gaps` list. The file is checked into main; regeneration is a required post-fix step. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: stale artifact must be regenerated as part of the fix; explorer reads it directly.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/` G-layer and L-layer JSON files \u2014 L0-forks.json, L1-trace-*.json (4 files), G1-G8.json files (8 files) \u2014 these are the source data fed into assemble_graph.py. None of the L1 trace entries carry any `attested`/`unattested` field or `verified: false` on individual transitions. None of the G-layer files (G1-G8) carry per-transition attestation status. This means the assembler cannot currently derive which transitions are unattested from the available data. Implementing gap detection either requires: (a) re-extracting L1 traces with a new `attested` boolean field per transition, or (b) implementing a heuristic within assemble_graph.py to infer unattested transitions from structural analysis (e.g., missing `source_file`/`source_heading` citations). | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: upstream data has no attestation markers; the implementation approach determines whether these files need re-extraction.\\n\\n### Tests\\n- No test files exist for `assemble_graph.py` in the pr-2626 branch or in the main branch. The gap edge generation logic has zero coverage. The `dh-workflow-graph.json` output schema is not validated anywhere in CI. There is no test that would catch a regression where `gap_count` is hardcoded rather than computed, or where `gap=True` is never set. | Risk: HIGH | Why: zero coverage for the feature being fixed; any regression in gap edge production would be invisible without tests.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md` \u2014 documents the `gap` edge type, the Gap list schema, and the assembler rule \\\"Every edge of type `gap` sets `gap: true` and `verified: false`\\\". This doc accurately describes the intended behavior but currently contradicts the implementation. After the fix, the doc will match the implementation and requires no content change. | Risk: LOW | Why: schema doc is forward-accurate; no update needed.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/COVERAGE.md` \u2014 notes \\\"Graph/explorer rebuild from L0+L1+G1-G8 | PENDING\\\". The rebuild step, when executed against a fixed assembler, will now produce gap edges. The COVERAGE.md entry should be updated after the rebuild to record that gap edges are now populated and the gap count is non-zero. | Risk: LOW | Why: informational manifest, no content becomes wrong before the rebuild; update needed after rebuild completes.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-trace-methodology.md` \u2014 references \\\"GAP rule: any transition not attested by a fragment is emitted as GAP\\\" in the context of the prior extraction run. Also notes that the 101 \\\"gaps\\\" in the earlier pass were file-reference expansion links misidentified as missing connections, not true unattested transitions. This distinction is important context for implementing the gap detection logic. Doc requires no update. | Risk: LOW | Why: describes methodology correctly; no content becomes stale.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n- None identified. No CI workflow validates `dh-workflow-graph.json` schema, tests assemble_graph.py, or checks gap_count against a minimum threshold. The committed `dh-workflow-graph.json` artifact is not schema-validated in any automated pipeline. No configuration files reference gap-edge behavior.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/workflow-extractor.md` \u2014 the upstream agent for all extraction passes (L0 forks, L1 traces, G1-G8 annotation layers). Its description says \\\"G1-G8 gap layers\\\" (referring to coverage-gap analysis layers, not the schema's gap edge type). Its \\\"HONEST GAPS\\\" rule says: \\\"If a field is not stated in the source, set it to null and set unverified: true.\\\" \u2014 this applies to data fields within extracted items, not to the schema's gap edge type. If the gap detection implementation requires L1 traces to carry an `attested: bool` field per transition, the workflow-extractor agent's instructions must be updated to extract and emit that field. If the fix is implemented entirely within assemble_graph.py using structural heuristics (e.g., missing `source_file` as the unattested signal), no agent update is needed. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: scope of agent update depends on implementation approach; agent is the sole upstream data producer.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/.claude/rules/workflow-extraction.md` \u2014 instructs all workflow-mapping, trace collection, and gap-layer passes to use `subagent_type=\\\"dh:workflow-extractor\\\"`. Mentions \\\"gap-layer pass\\\" as a pass type. Does not describe the schema's gap edge production. If a new extraction pass is added to collect attestation status per transition, this rule should reference it. | Risk: LOW | Why: rule is structurally correct; an update is only needed if a new pass type is added.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` | Role: producer | Connection: primary graph assembler \u2014 contains the broken `_edge(gap=False)` and hardcoded `gap_count=0` | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH | pattern: 'gap_count'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-graph.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: committed output artifact served to dh-workflow-explorer.html; must be regenerated after fix | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM | pattern: 'gap_count'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/dh-workflow-explorer.html` | Role: consumer | Connection: reads dh-workflow-graph.json; gap rendering infra fully implemented but permanently shows \\\"0 gaps\\\" | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW | pattern: 'gap_count'\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L0-forks.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: source data fed to assembler; no attested/unattested markers on transitions | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-work-backlog-item.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: L1 trace source data; no gap markers on transitions | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-complete-implementation.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: L1 trace source data; no gap markers on transitions | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-work-milestone.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: L1 trace source data; no gap markers on transitions | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/L1-trace-feature-skills.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: L1 trace source data; no gap markers on transitions | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G2-artifacts.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer fed to assembler; no per-transition attestation status | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G4-concurrency.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer; no attestation markers | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/G8-backend-routing.json` | Role: other-reference | Connection: annotation layer; no attestation markers | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/graph-schema.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: documents gap edge type and assembler output rules; accurate to intended behavior | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-layers/COVERAGE.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: tracks rebuild status as PENDING; must be updated after rebuild produces gap edges | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-trace-methodology.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: describes GAP rule and prior false-gap artifact; context for implementing detection logic | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/workflow-extractor.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: upstream data producer; may need instruction update if attested/unattested field is added to L1 traces | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/.claude/rules/workflow-extraction.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: governs extraction pass dispatch; may need update if new pass type is added | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- Tests (none) | Role: test | Connection: no test files exist for assemble_graph.py; gap edge generation has zero coverage | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n- Overall system risk: MEDIUM\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/docs/assemble_graph.py` \u2014 three-plane defect (edge flag never set, gap_count hardcoded, gaps list never populated), gap detection logic entirely absent, no test coverage\\n  - Tests (none) \u2014 zero coverage for the gap feature; any regression is invisible\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - verification (no tests)\\n  - migration (gap detection algorithm must be designed; upstream data lacks attested/unattested markers)\\n  - scope (implementation approach \u2014 structural heuristic vs. new extraction pass \u2014 changes whether L1 trace files and workflow-extractor agent also need changes)\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The upstream G-layer and L-layer JSON data files (10 files across L0, L1\u00d74, G1-G8) were not in the original item description but are directly implicated \u2014 gap detection cannot be implemented without understanding what attestation data is available in